1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,600
Hotel rooms and school wings sit empty for days.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:05,000
Before they're reused, their pipes get flushed.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,200
But a flush log only says a timer ran out.

4
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:12,200
It never proves the stagnant water was actually replaced.

5
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,900
That's the gap VeriFlush closes.

6
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,000
VeriFlush is a digital twin and controller.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,500
It tracks flow, volume and the temperature
at the end of the pipe,

8
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:25,800
then decides: Verified, Failed, or Uncertain.

9
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,000
Here, half the water escapes through a side branch.

10
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,800
The timer says done.

11
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:34,800
VeriFlush keeps going until the branch
is really exchanged.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,500
Across 520 simulated flushes,

13
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,200
timers passed stale water 37% of the time,
flow meters 31%.

14
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,800
VeriFlush: zero.

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
And this is our physical prototype:
the same sensors, logging live.

16
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,800
Dubai already requires weekly flushing.
VeriFlush turns that log into evidence.

17
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,500
Team Gurt. Making every flush count.
